# Six colours, each named on the tray under its paint in English, so the page reads in print.
lesson language-colours v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=7 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[colour.red|cap]], [[colour.blue]], [[colour.yellow]]"
  goal "Name six colours in [[language.name]]."

  look {
    scene 22x7 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        mixingtray paints wells=["red", "blue", "yellow", "green", "white", "black"] labels=true
      }
    }
    say """
      Colours: [[colour.red]] is red, [[colour.blue]] is blue, [[colour.yellow]] is yellow and
      [[colour.green]] is green. [[colour.white|cap]] is white and [[colour.black]] is black. The
      sea at the ferry town is blue, the houses are white and the sun is yellow: say each of them in
      the language.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.colours-name k=3
    }
    show language.colours-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.colours-meaning k=5 hard-k=0
    show language.colours-write k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    show language.colours-name k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.colours-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.colours-write k=4 hard-k=2
    show language.colours-meaning k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.colours-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.colours-write k=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.colours-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.colours-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[colour.red|cap]], [[colour.blue]], [[colour.yellow]], [[colour.green]], [[colour.white]], [[colour.black]]: red, blue, yellow, green, white, black."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is mixing [[colour.white]] and [[colour.black]], the opposites, and
    [[colour.blue]] and [[colour.green]]. The paint on the tray has its English name written under
    it, so on paper the question still reads: the child turns the English name into the language.
    The star questions use what a child knows about the world (the sun, the grass) rather than a
    colour on the page. [[colour.yellow|cap]] is said [[colour.yellow|say]]. If the child is stuck,
    go back to the look and say the six colours together, touching each paint. Off the page, sort a
    handful of crayons into colours and name each pile. It meets England's key stage 2 languages
    programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "broaden their vocabulary"; Japan's grades 3 and
    4 foreign language activities: "Activities to listen to simple words and phrases and basic
    expressions about their familiar people and their surroundings and match the phrases and
    expressions with illustrations and photographs"; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar
    everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[colour.white]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[colour.red]] and [[colour.blue]] and the words to write are [[colour.green]] and [[colour.yellow]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[colour.yellow]] and [[colour.black]] and the words to write are [[colour.white]], [[colour.green]] and [[colour.blue]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
